1
00:00:01,160 --> 00:00:02,040
Welcome back.

2
00:00:02,040 --> 00:00:04,863
Remember how I said there
wasn't one exact formula or

3
00:00:04,863 --> 00:00:08,430
template to follow when
conducting retrospectives?

4
00:00:08,430 --> 00:00:12,100
That's because every team learns,
adapts, and grows differently.

5
00:00:13,370 --> 00:00:16,650
In this video, we'll talk about
how to conduct a retrospective.

6
00:00:16,650 --> 00:00:20,720
Different situations call for different
tactics, and when it comes to receiving

7
00:00:20,720 --> 00:00:25,760
potentially sensitive feedback,
it's best to consider your team's needs.

8
00:00:25,760 --> 00:00:29,820
There are a couple of things to keep in
mind before you begin a retrospective.

9
00:00:29,820 --> 00:00:33,040
First, you'll want to maintain
a positive tone throughout the process.

10
00:00:34,090 --> 00:00:37,907
Remember, even if there are some
tough conversations, the objective of

11
00:00:37,907 --> 00:00:41,661
a retrospective is to encourage
improvements, which prepare you for

12
00:00:41,661 --> 00:00:43,360
future projects.

13
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:47,086
In general, the retrospective should be
considered a positive experience where

14
00:00:47,086 --> 00:00:49,680
team members feel comfortable
sharing their feedback.

15
00:00:51,340 --> 00:00:55,660
Next, you may need to be considerate
of teams outside of your own.

16
00:00:55,660 --> 00:00:58,024
If there are other teams
you partner with regularly,

17
00:00:58,024 --> 00:01:01,410
they'll need to be involved
in the retrospective, as well.

18
00:01:01,410 --> 00:01:02,161
For instance,

19
00:01:02,161 --> 00:01:06,031
some adjacent teams that were part of the
project may feel like they want to voice

20
00:01:06,031 --> 00:01:10,270
an opinion about the struggle to
maintain communication between teams.

21
00:01:10,270 --> 00:01:12,985
If they choose not to be
involved in the retrospective,

22
00:01:12,985 --> 00:01:16,240
you'll at least want to share
your findings with them.

23
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:18,958
After all,
smoothing cross-team interactions and

24
00:01:18,958 --> 00:01:22,890
deliverable handoffs is a frequent
topic of discussion in retrospectives.

25
00:01:23,910 --> 00:01:26,432
As previously mentioned,
you can use various props and

26
00:01:26,432 --> 00:01:29,150
tools to conduct your retrospective.

27
00:01:29,150 --> 00:01:32,110
Here's an example of what
a retrospective might entail.

28
00:01:33,470 --> 00:01:37,418
As you'll notice, it's pretty extensive
and includes a lot of opportunity for

29
00:01:37,418 --> 00:01:41,380
details. You'll want to encourage as much
feedback as possible from your team.

30
00:01:42,750 --> 00:01:47,150
This retrospective template is a standard
document, with room for project managers

31
00:01:47,151 --> 00:01:51,760
to fill out and discuss with their
teams and use it to guide the conversation.

32
00:01:51,760 --> 00:01:56,410
Go over the chain of events in the same
way that they happened in real time.

33
00:01:56,410 --> 00:01:58,330
What happened during the planning stage?

34
00:01:58,330 --> 00:02:00,130
What could have gone better?

35
00:02:00,130 --> 00:02:02,210
Where did your team get lucky?

36
00:02:02,210 --> 00:02:04,040
How about during the execution stage?

37
00:02:05,040 --> 00:02:08,578
As you do that, fill out the lessons
learned section, which is a space for

38
00:02:08,578 --> 00:02:12,370
you to elaborate on things you could
do differently the next time around.

39
00:02:13,600 --> 00:02:18,560
This is a space to include which risks
materialized during your project.

40
00:02:18,560 --> 00:02:22,560
Was there a large gap between
the original plan and its execution?

41
00:02:22,560 --> 00:02:24,460
How did the team feel about it?

42
00:02:24,460 --> 00:02:28,491
Maybe several of your project team members
commented on the fact that the website

43
00:02:28,491 --> 00:02:31,640
launch didn't meet the original deadline.

44
00:02:31,640 --> 00:02:36,490
Because of this, sales team members
didn't hit their numbers that month,

45
00:02:36,490 --> 00:02:41,006
the marketing department had to change the
date on several pieces of content and ads,

46
00:02:41,006 --> 00:02:45,100
and the sponsor had to answer to investors
who were eager to view the website.

47
00:02:46,260 --> 00:02:50,782
Team members are upset now because if you
had prioritized the website's completion

48
00:02:50,782 --> 00:02:54,740
and spent less time on less important
tasks, this may have been avoided.

49
00:02:55,990 --> 00:02:58,498
It's difficult feedback,
but it's pertinent for

50
00:02:58,498 --> 00:03:01,990
future successes to consider
why this risk materialised.

51
00:03:01,990 --> 00:03:06,690
Next time, you'll make sure to prioritize
a task with so many dependencies.

52
00:03:06,690 --> 00:03:10,190
Now that you've gone over how everything
went, build a better future for

53
00:03:10,190 --> 00:03:12,462
your team by filling out
the remaining tables.

54
00:03:12,462 --> 00:03:17,162
The first one is action items and will
address the question "What action should

55
00:03:17,163 --> 00:03:21,448
we take as a result of our lessons learned?" You'll start on the left-hand side

56
00:03:21,448 --> 00:03:25,873
with the action items you want to address,
then make your way through the cells to

57
00:03:25,873 --> 00:03:29,931
the right as you include information
like: type—as in, is this a tool,

58
00:03:29,931 --> 00:03:35,470
a process, a team, or something else?
Owner—who will own this action item?

59
00:03:35,471 --> 00:03:39,830
And relative links—consider where
we're tracking this item.

60
00:03:41,420 --> 00:03:44,590
The next table is all about
future considerations.

61
00:03:44,590 --> 00:03:47,792
Are there any risks that could
become issues if not addressed

62
00:03:47,792 --> 00:03:48,930
in the next quarter?

63
00:03:49,930 --> 00:03:53,560
Do you need to pass off ownership
of this project to someone else?

64
00:03:53,560 --> 00:03:57,980
Include those and make sure to fill
out the other cells, including:

65
00:03:57,981 --> 00:04:00,930
Type—is this a process,
a team, or something else?

66
00:04:00,930 --> 00:04:04,152
Contact—who can be a resource
on this procedure

67
00:04:04,152 --> 00:04:07,120
if we need to reference it later?

68
00:04:07,120 --> 00:04:11,117
And again, any links that may seem
relevant to this topic. that could be

69
00:04:11,117 --> 00:04:14,644
relevant documentation if you're
passing the project off or

70
00:04:14,644 --> 00:04:19,030
maybe a risk register from your
project. And there you have it.

71
00:04:19,030 --> 00:04:23,100
Now, I showed you a pretty standard
retrospective as you'd fill it out,

72
00:04:23,100 --> 00:04:26,298
but if you feel like your team needs
something a little more interactive,

73
00:04:26,298 --> 00:04:28,210
feel free to have fun with it.

74
00:04:28,210 --> 00:04:32,206
The way you gather this information from
your team can be more innovative than

75
00:04:32,206 --> 00:04:33,360
just a list.

76
00:04:33,360 --> 00:04:36,880
You can use color coding,
sticky notes, columns with emojis, and

77
00:04:36,880 --> 00:04:40,970
anything else you feel will suit
your team and keep them engaged.

78
00:04:40,970 --> 00:04:46,470
Whatever you do, make sure you carry the
lessons learned into your next projects.

79
00:04:46,470 --> 00:04:51,230
In the next video, we'll recap all of our
own lessons learned from this module.

80
00:04:51,230 --> 00:04:51,790
Meet you there!
